    "result_text": "Full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS). \n\n## Summary\n\nI added a `capacityWarning` flag that returns `true` when an owner's city already holds 40\u201349 owners (approaching the hard limit of 50, which is unchanged).\n\nBecause `GET /api/owners/{id}` re-maps from the persisted entity, the flag had to be stored on the owner (like the existing `membershipCap`), not just set on the POST response DTO. Changes, kept as small cohesive units:\n\n- **`CityCapacityWarning.java`** (new): a tiny cohesive helper \u2014 `isApproaching()` returns `count >= 40 && count < CityCapacity.LIMIT`, reusing the existing `LIMIT` constant so the hard-limit boundary stays single-sourced.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`**: one added line \u2014 `owner.setCapacityWarning(CityCapacityWarning.isApproaching(...))`, mirroring the adjacent `setMembershipCap` line. No new branch, so the method's complexity is unchanged.\n- **`Owner.java`**: added the persisted `capacityWarning` field with getter/setter, mirroring `membershipCap`; MapStruct auto-maps it by name, so the mapper was untouched.\n- **`openapi.yml`**: declared the `capacityWarning` boolean (read-only, default false), which generates the DTO setter.\n- **`schema.sql` / `data.sql`**: added the `capacity_warning` column and the matching positional value in the seed inserts.\n\nI deliberately delegated the logic to a fresh, near-zero-complexity class and touched `addOwner` with a single non-branching line to keep the change off the large controller/entity classes.",
